ikipedia/en/d/d3/Psarou_beach.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Ftelia","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":37.461111,"lo":25.375,"d":4480,"ds":"Ftelia (Greek: Φτελιά) is an archaeological site located on a beach on the island of Mykonos, Greece.","wpu":"Ftelia","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Ftelia_on_Mykonos%2C_Early_Cycladic_settlement%2C_060361.jpg/960px-Ftelia_on_Mykonos%2C_Early_Cycladic_settlement%2C_060361.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Marmaronisi","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":37.492222,"lo":25.366667,"d":6036,"ds":"Marmaronisi (Greek: Μαρμαρονήσι) is a small uninhabited rock island on the northern side of Mykonos in the Cyclades. It is located at the entrance of the bay of Panormos and opposite the settlement of Agios Sostis. Marmaronisi is named for the composition of the rocks of the island, which is also suitable for manufacturing lime.","wpu":"Marmaronisi","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Marmaronisi.jpg/960px-Marmaronisi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Archaeological Museum of Delos","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":37.400833,"lo":25.269167,"d":7320,"ds":"The Archaeological Museum of Delos (Greek: Αρχαιολογικό Μουσείο Δήλου) is a museum on the island of Delos, near Mykonos in the South Aegean, Greece. It is noted for its extensive collection of statues unearthed in the surrounding area of the ancient site, which has been declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site.","wpu":"Archaeological Museum of Delos","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Exterior_del_Museu_Arqueol%C3%B2gic_de_Delos.JPG/960px-Exterior_del_Museu_Arqueol%C3%B2gic_de_Delos.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Minoan Fountain","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":37.40124,"lo":25.26792,"d":7365,"ds":"The Minoan Fountain was a monumental public fountain hewn directly from the natural rock. It is located directly behind the Stoa of Antigonos outside the temenos of the Sanctuary of Apollo on the island of Delos. The fountainhouse formalized the use of the sacred spring, and was dedicated to the Cult of the Nymphs.","wpu":"Minoan Fountain","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Minoan_Fountain%2C_Delos.jpg/960px-Minoan_Fountain%2C_Delos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Cynthus","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":37.395278,"lo":25.2725,"d":7584,"ds":"Mount Cynthus (Greek: Κύνθος, romanized: Kýnthos) is located on the isle of Delos, part of the Greek Cyclades.","wpu":"Cynthus","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Mount_Cynthus%2C_Delos.jpg/960px-Mount_Cynthus%2C_Delos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Agora of the Competaliasts","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":37.399,"lo":25.2663,"d":7643,"ds":"The Agora of the Competaliasts (Greek: Αγορά των Κομπεταλιαστών) is an ancient Greek archaeological site on the island of Delos, Greece, which dates to the last quarter of the 2nd century BC. It is an agora (public space and commercial center) directly adjacent to the Sacred Harbour. The bases of a square and a round marble monument, both dedicated to Hermes, can be found in the center of the market square.","wpu":"Agora of the Competaliasts","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/GR-delos-agora-compitaliasten.jpg/960px-GR-delos-agora-compitaliasten.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Delos","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":37.393333,"lo":25.271111,"d":7830,"ds":"Delos or Dilos (; Greek: Δήλος [ˈðilos]; Attic Greek: Δῆλος Dêlos, Doric Greek: Δᾶλος Dâlos), is a small Greek island near Mykonos, close to the centre of the Cyclades archipelago. Though only 3.43 km2 (1.32 sq mi) in area, it is one of the most important mythological, historical, and archaeological sites in Greece.","wpu":"Delos","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Delos_general.jpg/960px-Delos_general.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Rineia","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":37.409,"lo":25.229,"d":9679,"ds":"Rineia, or Rhenea (Ρήνεια), is a Greek island in the Cyclades. It lies just west of the island of Delos and further southwest of the island of Mykonos, of which it and Delos are administratively a part. Its area is 14 km2 (5 sq mi).","wpu":"Rineia","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Rineia.jpg/960px-Rineia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Mykonos (, UK also ; Greek: Μύκονος [ˈmikonos]) is a Greek island, part of the Cyclades, lying between Tinos, Syros, Paros and Naxos. The island has an area of 85.5 square kilometres (33.0 sq mi) and rises to an elevation of 341 metres (1,119 feet) at its highest point. At the 2021 census, there were 10,704 inhabitants, most of whom lived in the largest town, Mykonos, which is on the west coast.","wpu":"Mykonos","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/2011_Dimos_Mykonou.png/960px-2011_Dimos_Mykonou.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}